// The workspace the Aletheia Go modules are developed in.
//
// The loader that reads spreadsheets is a module of its own, ./excel, so that
// excelize and what it brings with it are optional to a consumer who wants the
// core binding alone. The command line, ./cmd/aletheia, is a module of its own
// for the same reason: its template command writes through the loader.
//
// A module the use directive names is the one the workspace builds, whatever
// version another module's require names, but Go still reads that version's
// go.mod. The replaces below give it the tree's, so the workspace resolves
// with no network: without one, Go asks the network for that version, which an
// offline build refuses. Each replace is written here rather than in
// the requiring module's own file because a relative path written there would
// travel with the module. Each names the same version the requires do, a
// version-specific replace matching nothing when the two drift apart.
//
// Outside the workspace each module builds against the versions it requires,
// which the sweep's "go build (outside the workspace)" step holds: a module
// coming to use what a required version lacks moves that requirement to a
// later commit on main, with `go get <module>@<commit>` run under GOWORK=off
// in the requiring module, and its replace here moves with it. A release
// moves every requirement on the core and Excel modules to its own version,
// and the replaces with them, through `python -m tools.go_release write`.
//
// This file is not part of the bundle a release builds, which carries the core
// module's files alone, and a consumer of any module never sees it.
go 1.26.0

use (
	.
	./cmd/aletheia
	./excel
)

replace github.com/Jaetan/aletheia/go/v5 v5.0.0-20261120132838-2150dabcfa97 => ./

replace github.com/Jaetan/aletheia/go/excel v0.0.0-20261120222537-2b12b86d9f6a => ./excel
